 "source_notes": "All values from the Bowen National Research PDF (read via WebFetch; each gap number read at least twice with different prompts; no other sources used). Gaps: table 'Housing Gaps by Tenure and County - State of North Carolina (2024 to 2029)', pp. II-15/16; per tenure the columns are gap units, share of state, and 'Gap to Renter/Owner/Total Households Ratio' (stored as extra gap_to_*_households_pct fields; the total ratio equals total gap / 2029 projected households, NOT 2024). Households: table 'Total Households', pp. III-22/23, columns '2024 Estimated', '2029 Projected', 'Change 2024-2029 Percent' (hh_growth_pct). rental_gap_by_ami: 'Overall Rental Housing Gaps by County' pp. V-6/7, bands by Percent of Area Median Income (<=30%, 31%-50%, 51%-80%, 81%-120%, 121%-150%, 151%+), cross-checked against Addendum A '[County] County Housing Gap Estimates by Income (2024 to 2029)' row 'Total Rental Housing Gaps'. forsale_gap_by_price: 'Overall For-Sale Housing Gaps by County' pp. V-14/15 (same AMI bands) with each band's 'Price Range' row from the county's Addendum A table (pp. A-9 to A-12); the report publishes for-sale gaps by AMI band and the price range is the affordable home price for that band. multifamily_vacancy_pct: 'Surveyed Multifamily Rental Housing Supply by County' pp. IV-3 to IV-5, column 'Overall Vacancy Rate'; null where the report shows a dash because 0 projects were surveyed (Jones, Lenoir, Lincoln, Martin, Northampton, Onslow, Pamlico, Pender, Perquimans); several non-null values rest on tiny samples (e.g. Mitchell 1 project/24 units, Pasquotank 1/180, Madison 2/82). forsale_availability_pct and median_list_price: 'Available For-Sale Housing by County' (as of early July 2024) pp. IV-37/38, columns 'Availability Rate' and 'Median List Price'. Checks passed for all 23: rental+forsale=total; AMI bands sum to tenure totals; growth pct and total-gap ratio consistent with household counts. Caveats: households, vacancy, availability and median list price were each read once (but pass arithmetic checks where one exists); Mecklenburg and Orange lowest price-range labels came back without the '<=' sign, which was added here.",
